WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:03.940
This one is actually a mold, so
it has, like, a texture that--

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.330
Oh, yeah.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.609
My favorite is the
purple and the yellow.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:08.109
OK.

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:11.310
[WARBLING]

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.570
We're using bacteria
as, basically,

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.880
an agent to paint with.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.190
You can manipulate
the DNA of bacteria.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.200
I want to use bacteria
as a detection

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.600
agent for toxins
in the environment

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.780
or for the detection of cancer.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.990
In a scientific world,
the focus is actually

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:33.920
on the applications of this
material, idea, relationship.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.900
As an artist, I'm
more interested

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.170
in the philosophical,
representational, or even

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.107
interpretational level.

00:00:41.107 --> 00:00:42.440
Do they know what they're going?

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.830
[WARBLING]

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.230
The Visiting Artist
Program really

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:59.520
became active in the 1970s,
but even before that,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.190
you really have to go
back to Gyorgy Kepes, who

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.200
came over from the Bauhaus.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.680
He landed here and he said, oh,
I'm in a different environment.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.750
This is an environment
full of scientists,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.550
full of engineers,
full of people who are

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.500
trying to invent new things.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.390
I really think the
artists and the scientists

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.910
have a lot to say
to one another.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:22.710
He understood how the arts
could fit in at an institution

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.090
of MIT'S quality.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.240
And by the late '60s, he
had created the Center

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.455
for Advanced Visual Studies.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:37.670
The artists who were here were
not satisfied to just make art.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.280
They wanted to develop
new technologies,

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.040
so it just came as a
kind of natural thing,

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.333
to create a visiting
artist program to enhance

00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:46.875
this culture.

00:01:46.875 --> 00:01:50.128
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.080
The kinds of artists we
wish to bring to our campus

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.380
are artists who have a
feel in the way they create

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.980
the art that intersects
with science and technology,

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:07.030
and to learn from us
as we learn from them.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.460
If you could go
into fantasy land

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:14.140
and describe what would
be the ideal studio,

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.990
for any artist in
the 21st century,

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:19.480
it would be MIT facilities.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.240
A lot of my work is trying to
figure out interesting ways

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.070
to combine materials
with computers,

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.560
and through the research, I
found out about Vik's work.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.750
They sent Vik an email.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.160
Immediately, he
responded, and he

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.840
was like, yes, I
want to come by,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.210
I've heard about MIT and the
Media Lab for a long time.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:37.810
This is a highly abstracted
image, because what's going on

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.710
is, that system there is
throwing charged particles

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.600
of gallium at the surface.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.720
Vik has this vision of etching
castles into grains of sand.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.470
And he asked, like,
can we do this at MIT?

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.428
Is there a way that we
can make an image that's

00:02:52.428 --> 00:02:54.867
so small that we can
actually put it onto a grain?

00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:56.950
We've been going through
all these different kinds

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.920
of equipment, creating really
cool techniques for etching it,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.260
and then imaging it as well.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:04.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.510
Science can benefit
enormously from interacting

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.510
with people who are
actually concerned about how

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.880
to represent the physical
world in a way that

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.250
is accessible but
also understandable.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:23.845
4, 3, 2, 1, 0, and liftoff.

00:03:23.845 --> 00:03:27.058
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.310
Trevor Paglen came to
us with this project

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:33.370
where he wanted to put
a plaque on a satellite

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.790
in a geostationary orbit around
the Earth and, very likely,

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.760
outlive the planet.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.940
How can we encode
information into materials

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:46.670
that can last for very,
very long periods of time?

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.390
These intersections between
deep time and human time

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:52.750
are very interesting
to think about.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:58.270
He decided to put a photographic
archive of major events

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:03.660
in the life of the
Earthlings of this planet.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.920
This is not something
I can do in my studio,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.520
this is not something
I can do with materials

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.610
from my local art supply store.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.100
This is the kind of
project that is really

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.440
only possible at a research
institution like MIT.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.516
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.190
What's really important about
the Visiting Artist Program

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.590
here is that it's
based on collaboration

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:32.550
with our existing
creative arts faculty.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.620
Joan Jonas, our
performance artist

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.800
in the program in art,
culture, and technology,

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.060
was already collaborating with
the young jazz pianist Jason

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.210
Mraz, a MacArthur Award
winner, a brilliant pianist.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.462
[PLAYING PAINO]

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:55.020
We brought Jason here
to MIT to work with Joan

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.602
to workshop a new
performance piece

00:04:57.602 --> 00:04:58.810
by Joan called "Reanimation."

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.750
[PLAYING PAINO]

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.260
The tone and the
volume have to--

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.370
Don Byron is a
brilliant jazz composer.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.260
You guys really sound amazing.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:15.240
We brought him back to MIT,
where he worked on a new piece

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.530
for a gospel choir.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:17.322
[MUSIC - "HOSANNA"]

00:05:17.322 --> 00:05:21.010
(SINGING) We can't sing
without you, Hosanna, Hosanna.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.070
And then, he composed
a concerto for clarinet

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:28.730
for Evan Ziporyn, which was
performed with the student wind

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:29.230
ensemble.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:32.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.030
It's just a musical
feast, quite literally.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:37.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:39.650
They had quite a
rapport, the two of them.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.690
Students need to learn how
to play things in rhythm

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:46.470
and make them feel good.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:50.630
So, I think I made the
thing rhythmically difficult

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:51.155
on purpose.

00:05:51.155 --> 00:05:54.386
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:59.207 --> 00:06:00.790
My name is [? Ravello ?]
[INAUDIBLE],,

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:03.670
and I thought that
this morning would

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.380
be, maybe,
interesting to explain

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.450
to you, my method of working.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.600
Putting students in
contact with people

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.410
who are at the
top of their game,

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:14.980
whether it's in science, or
engineering, or the arts,

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:18.760
and having them learn by
creating alongside them

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:22.870
is a very special part of
MIT's educational philosophy.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:25.935
There's no rules to this,
you just draw the impressions

00:06:25.935 --> 00:06:26.560
that you have--

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.260
The McDermott Award is one
of the highest level artist

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.060
awards in the world.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.790
In all cases, the
people who get the award

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:39.270
spend some time at MIT with
the students in some classes.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.970
It's more than an
honor, it's also

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:46.050
an opportunity to benefit
from the creative culture

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:47.280
here on campus.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:48.240
Any questions?

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:51.120
Any doubts, any fears?

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.520
When you're starting out,
you're an amateur at this,

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.740
you don't know what you're
doing is going to go off

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.240
in the right direction.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.490
But then having someone
who's done it all their life

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.460
and is successful at it coming
to MIT, and working with you,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.480
and making you feel
like you're part

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.520
of the process-- once you
feel that, you definitely

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:06.780
have a chance doing good work.

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:12.620
But don't be nervous.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:15.330
[VOCALIZING]

00:07:20.544 --> 00:07:22.166
You know?

00:07:22.166 --> 00:07:23.020
Four, and.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.980
The young conductor of the
Los Angeles Philharmonic,

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.420
Gustavo Dudamel, came
here to receive the Award.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:31.990
Need an audience to--

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:34.960
Robert Lepage, the
Canadian theater maestro--

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:36.340
everything, he does it all.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:39.280
With experience, we've
learned that we should not

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.540
force connections.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:43.580
We let them, and eventually,
two or three things

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:46.190
seems to go together, and we
go, oh, there's a pattern there.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:47.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:52.210
I've never really seen the,
quote unquote, "division"

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:55.540
between the arts and sciences
as any kind of dichotomy.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.660
To be a complete
person, it makes sense

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.450
to be educated at both.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:05.740
So having a vibrant arts
program at a school like MIT

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.080
is important for making
complete human beings,

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.950
for making human beings
that connect to the world.

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:15.310
Asking questions that
haven't been asked before.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:18.310
When you put the
artist into the mix,

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:20.680
I think those questions
become larger,

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.620
they become more
speculative, and they also

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.300
have this human dimension.

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.110
This one is a mistake.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:31.540
It's not an actual experiment,
but it still looks really cool.

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:33.460
Working with Vik
has really made me

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:37.570
think about the whole
cycle of technology and art

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:40.120
and how those two
are interwoven.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:43.330
And it's just a beautiful
cycle, connecting nature

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:46.200
and the high technology
that we practice here.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.700
It would be really interesting
to film, on a microscope, all

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:49.570
the details there.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:52.120
There's a lot of
intricate stuff going on,

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:53.650
like, some bacteria
are swimming,

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.840
and other bacteria are just
climbing on top of each other

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:57.048
and they like to communicate.

00:08:57.048 --> 00:08:59.992
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:38.520
I.

